# Hand-chosen tree-construction cases for the HTML tree builder, in the .dat format of the
# web-platform-tests vectors (the format is described in third_party/html/wpt/.../README.md).
#
# Why they exist: the WPT vectors are what the standard's authors wrote, and a planted
# defect (tools/conformance/run-html-tree.sh --controls) showed rules they never exercise:
# where the adoption agency algorithm's bookmark goes, `ol` and `ul` as the boundaries of
# list item scope, the scopes of `button` and `marquee`. Each case below is one that fails
# when its rule is undone. The expected trees are NOT ours: they are what html5lib 1.1 and
# parse5 8.0.1 both answer (tools/oracle/html_tree_extra_check.py asks them again and fails
# if either has changed or they no longer agree), and the tree builder agrees with both.
# Cases where a reference is older than the standard (select, ruby, templates) or lacks a feature
# (processing instructions) are left out. No #errors section is compared for this file.
#
# The last thirteen (from `<table><caption><b>x</caption>y</table>z`) are stage 3d's: the table modes and foster
# parenting. Each fails when one of the new rules is undone (the plants in run-html-tree.sh): the caption, the
# column group, the cell and the caption closing the list of active formatting elements, the implied sections,
# foster parenting of text and of elements, the formatting elements reopened after a table, the adoption agency
# moving a block in front of a table. (Templates are not here: html5lib 1.1 reads them by older rules.)
#
# Stage 3e (foreign content and fragments) are the cases from `<svg><g><b>` on (61).
# Each case fails when one of the new rules is undone (the plants in run-html-tree.sh): the breakout list
# (six cases, the 44 names in groups of seven, each as `<svg><g><name>`), the attributes that make a font
# break out, the formatting elements reopened before an svg or math element, the foreign end tag's walk
# (past integration points, across namespaces, not past an HTML element), the special foreign elements
# (their end tags and list items are stopped by them), CDATA, the attribute adjustments, and fragments (the
# contexts of the algorithm: the tokenizer state, the template modes, the select and frameset contexts, a
# foreign context). A case in an SVG or MathML context is answered by parse5 alone (html5lib's API cannot take
# one), and html_tree_extra_check.py checks that it still cannot. The reference-only gaps are not here: a case
# a reference answers differently is a witness of tools/oracle/html_tree_diff.py instead (the integration
# points as special elements in html5lib, the noscript context with scripting off, feDropShadow).

